Track 1: Cardboard Refractors Bruising Jupiter
- Story & Context: It began on the tar-sticky roof of the Majestic Roxy Cinema in June 2003, where Kabir Varma dragged an obsolete 35mm Simplex projector beside three rusted amateur cardboard telescopes pointed drunkenly at the gas giants. The machine spat blank, unexposed leader film into the salt breeze, projecting a blinding square of white against the coastal sea-fog while the quartet attempted to score an imaginary 1924 silent shipwreck film that existed only in Kabir's sleep-deprived brain. When the searing carbon-arc lamp suddenly ignited a dry pigeon nest inside the vent pipe, showering the sitar in orange sparks and the sharp vinegar stench of melting acetate, the band broke into a panicked 210-BPM swing stampede instead of fleeing. Over the roar of the burning booth, Kabir screamed through an overdriven bullet harmonica mic, "Strike the carbon rod and let the planets burn!", ripping a microtonal sitar blast that permanently welded hard-bop changes to saltwater surf feedback.
