Track 2: "Who Scratched the Aperture on Rigel?"
- Story & Context: High on the fire-escape landing above Topanga Lookout, drummer Trixie De La Cruz nursed split knuckles bound in gaffer tape, washing down the taste of flat guava soda with sea spray as she confessed to sabotaging the local multiplex with sugar water in the projector cogs. She had smashed her father's brass refractor to salvage its cracked concave focal mirror, bolting it directly to her hi-hat stand so every foot-splash blasted blinding silver sun-flares across the band’s makeshift stage. The resulting track unfolds as a heart-scraped character study where weeping wah-wah sitar melodies duel with aggressive sax double-tonguing, scoring a non-existent 1921 melodrama about two deep-sea astronomers trapped in an airlock. Leaning into the room mic as the tempo accelerated toward boiling point, Trixie yelled the confessional refrain, "My hands are full of glass and yesterday's celluloid!", turning her optical vandalism into high-speed rhythmic ecstasy.
