Track 3: Chitra-Chop Over the Zenith
- Story & Context: By late August, seaside lore claimed that over two hundred runaway projectionists and beachcombers gathered atop the decaying pilings of Shutter Pier, aiming every salvaged camera lens in the county straight up at the zenith to catch the Perseid meteors. When coastal patrol boats approached with sirens wailing to cut the generator cables, saxophonist Orson Bale shoved his bell into an old megaphonic horn and blew until he tasted copper, refusing to surrender the final reel of their phantom silent epic. The rhythm section answered with a cataclysmic collision of roaring surf tremolo, marimba arpeggios, and modal sitar ragas, sustaining the sound until the projector's lamp exploded from the sea dampness in an emerald flash that blinded the docks. As the mist settled and the sirens reached the roof, the stage was empty—leaving behind only smoking tube amplifiers, a pool of saltwater, and spray-painted words humming in the warm breeze: "Roll the blank leader, the tide is our screen!"
